WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.329 --> 00:00:03.600
A streak of warm blood splattered across the
snow.

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:06.819
Steam rose off it as it fizzled into the ice
crystals below.

00:00:06.819 --> 00:00:11.610
The severed leg it’d just spurted from lay
twitching only a few inches away, torn clean

00:00:11.610 --> 00:00:13.730
off at the hip like a broken Barbie doll.

00:00:13.730 --> 00:00:15.880
I think it might’ve belonged to Arthur.

00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:18.119
It wasn’t the first time I’d seen limbs
cut off.

00:00:18.119 --> 00:00:20.330
Hell, I’ve cut a few off myself in my time.

00:00:20.330 --> 00:00:23.920
Don’t worry, I’m not a serial killer - Just
a med student.

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:24.920
We all were.

00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:28.380
I couldn’t say the same for the nightmare
we encountered up in the mountains that terrible

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:29.380
day.

00:00:29.380 --> 00:00:31.689
The thin, cold air was laden with screams.

00:00:31.689 --> 00:00:33.900
Mine, my friends’, and that...Thing’s.

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:37.380
All that it’d done to us.

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:39.200
All that it was still doing to us.

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:44.380
And never once did it stop shrieking and sobbing
at the top of its lungs, like it wasn’t

00:00:44.380 --> 00:00:46.180
even in control of its actions.

00:00:46.180 --> 00:00:47.700
It didn’t want to do this.

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:48.700
It just had to.

00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:51.140
This violence was as inevitable as the sunset.

00:00:51.140 --> 00:00:52.540
I screamed for Kyle.

00:00:52.540 --> 00:00:57.080
The snow was coming down thick and fast, a
curtain of burning, brilliant white.

00:00:57.080 --> 00:01:00.550
I couldn’t see anyone, not even the thing
that was killing us.

00:01:00.550 --> 00:01:02.730
The water in my eyes was starting to freeze
up.

00:01:02.730 --> 00:01:04.180
It was cold.

00:01:04.180 --> 00:01:05.690
So damn cold.

00:01:05.690 --> 00:01:07.830
What the hell was going on here?

00:01:07.830 --> 00:01:11.380
Suddenly, a dark shape was moving towards
me through the wall of falling snow.

00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:12.860
It was human.

00:01:12.860 --> 00:01:13.860
Vaguely.

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:15.530
Moving towards me with frantic speed.

00:01:15.530 --> 00:01:19.420
I tensed in anticipation when I then saw Kyle
emerging from the fog.

00:01:19.420 --> 00:01:21.010
He was splattered with blood.

00:01:21.010 --> 00:01:23.430
His face red from screaming and exertion.

00:01:23.430 --> 00:01:26.310
His eyes pleaded for help I couldn’t give
him.

00:01:26.310 --> 00:01:31.341
The horror caused my body to react on auto-pilot
with my eyes clamming closed in terror, all

00:01:31.341 --> 00:01:35.420
I could do was open my mouth, point with a
shaking, frostbitten finger, and force out

00:01:35.420 --> 00:01:36.440
a scream:

00:01:36.440 --> 00:01:37.590
“Behind you!”

00:01:37.590 --> 00:01:42.680
My words were carried off by the brutal mountain
winds, and with the sounds of mutilation ahead

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:48.720
of me, all I could imagine with eyes clamped
closed as I hit the frozen ground was a long,

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:55.420
grey arm emerging from the flurry behind Kyle,
its gnarled, clawed fingers reaching for his

00:01:55.420 --> 00:01:56.420
head...

00:01:56.420 --> 00:01:58.610
It was meant to be the vacation of a lifetime.

00:01:58.610 --> 00:02:02.320
The kind of thing that you could only experience
once, but you’d never forget.

00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:06.700
We all thought, in our own ways, that we’d
look back on the photos with misty eyes when

00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:07.970
we were all old men.

00:02:07.970 --> 00:02:12.740
We didn’t know that, within a week of arriving
in Switzerland, whether any of us would even

00:02:12.740 --> 00:02:16.800
make it to old age would suddenly become an
open question.

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:19.090
All four of us were doctors in training.

00:02:19.090 --> 00:02:22.890
Even if you don’t know much about the profession,
it probably won’t surprise you to hear that

00:02:22.890 --> 00:02:26.740
there’s a lot of practice and education
involved before you can ever really call yourself

00:02:26.740 --> 00:02:28.690
a doctor without feeling like a fraud.

00:02:28.690 --> 00:02:33.630
You need to ace your SATs, then slog through
four years of pre-med, another four years

00:02:33.630 --> 00:02:37.860
of medical school, then the better part of
a decade doing your residency.

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:42.340
Seems a little much, I know, but believe me,
you don’t want some cowboy sticking his

00:02:42.340 --> 00:02:45.450
hands in your chest cavity without all the
proper training.

00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:50.170
But after we finally graduated from Harvard
Med, my friends and I decided that we needed

00:02:50.170 --> 00:02:53.670
a break before the profession swallowed us
back up again.

00:02:53.670 --> 00:02:58.110
It was me, Brandon, Arthur, and of course,
Kyle.

00:02:58.110 --> 00:03:01.720
A lot of people I attended class with would
probably make a tidy sum over the course of

00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:03.220
their medical careers.

00:03:03.220 --> 00:03:05.349
Many of them would have made fine doctors.

00:03:05.349 --> 00:03:10.480
But all of us knew that Kyle Molloy was more
than that - He was going to be a truly great

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:11.480
doctor.

00:03:11.480 --> 00:03:16.860
He was smart, confident, driven, and had the
kind of smooth, effortless charm that made

00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:19.150
you just feel safe around him.

00:03:19.150 --> 00:03:21.099
Where he went, we followed.

00:03:21.099 --> 00:03:25.500
So naturally, when he suggested we take a
week-long sojourn in Switzerland, the rest

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:26.989
of us were all for it.

00:03:26.989 --> 00:03:32.030
We expected an easy week of gourmet cheese,
fine chocolate, and the occasional jaunt down

00:03:32.030 --> 00:03:34.270
some old historic districts.

00:03:34.270 --> 00:03:38.540
The exact kind of relaxing break we needed
after the soul-crushing pressure of eight

00:03:38.540 --> 00:03:41.440
years studying the hallowed art of medicine.

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:44.850
But of course, that wasn’t what Kyle had
planned for us.

00:03:44.850 --> 00:03:50.020
It turned out that his tastes were far more
extreme than what the rest of us had in mind.

00:03:50.020 --> 00:03:55.000
His vision of a Swiss vacation was mostly
forest hikes, backcountry skiing, snowboarding,

00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:56.959
and a little mountaineering through the Swiss
Alps.

00:03:56.959 --> 00:04:01.410
Apparently, he’d spent a good portion of
his childhood taking Swiss vacations, and

00:04:01.410 --> 00:04:04.800
wanted to give us a little slice of his personal
paradise.

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:09.769
As someone who wasn’t exactly crazy about
heights or the cold, I can’t pretend I was

00:04:09.769 --> 00:04:11.050
eager at first.

00:04:11.050 --> 00:04:13.940
But Kyle always found a way of talking us
back around.

00:04:13.940 --> 00:04:18.499
It wasn’t long until we were booking our
tickets, then sitting on the plane, watching

00:04:18.499 --> 00:04:22.850
the mountains get bigger through the porthole,
then our feet were on Swiss soil.

00:04:22.850 --> 00:04:26.490
It was a beautiful country, and every bit
as cold as I feared.

00:04:26.490 --> 00:04:30.509
But soon enough, the cold would be the absolute
least of our worries.

00:04:30.509 --> 00:04:34.749
As I glanced out over the breathtaking view
of the Alps, I had no idea of the terrible

00:04:34.749 --> 00:04:39.930
nightmare lurking out there, hidden in the
dark spaces of those great rocks.

00:04:39.930 --> 00:04:43.499
Something I learned in med school is that
human beings are kind of like fleshy time

00:04:43.499 --> 00:04:45.879
bombs, their fuse ever-burning.

00:04:45.879 --> 00:04:50.509
When you reach a certain age - and believe
me, that age is younger than you think - the

00:04:50.509 --> 00:04:55.949
assassin that will one day sneak up from within
and take your life is already waiting inside

00:04:55.949 --> 00:04:56.949
you.

00:04:56.949 --> 00:05:01.210
The cluster of malformed cells that will one
day turn into an inoperable malignant tumor.

00:05:01.210 --> 00:05:05.759
The early whispers of the heart defect that
gives your ticker its planned obsolescence.

00:05:05.759 --> 00:05:10.860
The microscopic ropes of defective genes that’ll
one day turn into Alzheimer’s or Parkinson’s.

00:05:10.860 --> 00:05:15.060
In some cases, it’s just a matter of whether
you can catch it in time.

00:05:15.060 --> 00:05:18.840
But in just as many cases, there really is
nothing you can do.

00:05:18.840 --> 00:05:20.960
You’re just waiting for it to get you.

00:05:20.960 --> 00:05:24.839
After three days in Switzerland, we were suiting
up in our thick coats and climbing boots,

00:05:24.839 --> 00:05:28.369
having no idea that the same principle applied
to us.

00:05:28.369 --> 00:05:33.740
Except this wasn’t that nasty little assassin
within - it was something outside.

00:05:33.740 --> 00:05:37.229
Something that none of our skills or knowledge
could save us from.

00:05:37.229 --> 00:05:40.430
Something very old and very dangerous.

00:05:40.430 --> 00:05:44.659
We were in fate’s hands now, and sometimes,
fate can be cruel.

00:05:44.659 --> 00:05:45.800
Kyle was steaming ahead.

00:05:45.800 --> 00:05:50.930
He didn’t just want us to ascend just any
mountain peak, he wanted us to attempt the

00:05:50.930 --> 00:05:55.939
Dufourspitze, the highest peak of the Monte
Rosa massif, and the Alps as a whole.

00:05:55.939 --> 00:05:59.289
Kyle didn’t think anything less than that
would be a challenge.

00:05:59.289 --> 00:06:03.330
After all, this was our once in a lifetime
vacation before we fully started our residency.

00:06:03.330 --> 00:06:06.949
Didn’t we want to make this a truly remarkable
experience?

00:06:06.949 --> 00:06:10.150
And because it was Kyle, none of us disagreed.

00:06:10.150 --> 00:06:13.639
We started at the base of the Monte Rosa and
began to rise.

00:06:13.639 --> 00:06:19.080
Its lowest levels barely resembled its frosty
peak, over 4,600 feet above.

00:06:19.080 --> 00:06:23.930
We took pictures of some of the wildlife:
The grazing Ibexes, Red Deer, and Chamois.

00:06:23.930 --> 00:06:27.569
Arthur had bought a pamphlet on the local
wildlife back at a tourist-friendly mountain

00:06:27.569 --> 00:06:31.809
lodge and took great joy in pointing out the
different creatures to us as he saw them.

00:06:31.809 --> 00:06:35.659
Brandon got a good laugh about the fact there
was a small bird native to the area known

00:06:35.659 --> 00:06:37.159
as the Nuthatch.

00:06:37.159 --> 00:06:40.990
Kyle forged ahead, telling tales of his prior
conquests.

00:06:40.990 --> 00:06:45.500
The climb gradually transitioned from calm
to grueling as we ascended the mountain.

00:06:45.500 --> 00:06:50.189
I could feel the blisters forming on my feet
as the cold first made my face sting, and

00:06:50.189 --> 00:06:51.719
then go completely numb.

00:06:51.719 --> 00:06:56.779
Arthur, still consulting his pamphlet, expressed
excitement to see a marmot when we finally

00:06:56.779 --> 00:06:59.309
reached 2000 feet of elevation.

00:06:59.309 --> 00:07:03.830
Brandon started to complain vocally, pouring
himself a cup of coffee from his thermos.

00:07:03.830 --> 00:07:06.449
Kyle chastised him for wimping out already.

00:07:06.449 --> 00:07:09.330
After all, he said, the hardest parts were
yet to come.

00:07:09.330 --> 00:07:13.009
Oh, if only he knew just how right he was...

00:07:13.009 --> 00:07:17.750
We ascended further, feeling the air thinning
and our legs growing exhausted beneath us.

00:07:17.750 --> 00:07:21.270
I started to resent Kyle as he forged on,
despite it all.

00:07:21.270 --> 00:07:23.889
How could he keep going when the rest of us
were faltering?

00:07:23.889 --> 00:07:25.629
It just wasn’t fair.

00:07:25.629 --> 00:07:30.449
When the rest of us needed to take a breather,
we could feel the sting of his silent judgment.

00:07:30.449 --> 00:07:32.149
He was always so damn perfect.

00:07:32.149 --> 00:07:36.869
“We’re almost there,” he kept saying,
even though we all knew it wasn’t true.

00:07:36.869 --> 00:07:39.399
Things took a turn during one of our rest
stops.

00:07:39.399 --> 00:07:43.990
We heard this strange noise, a kind of pained
wailing or keening.

00:07:43.990 --> 00:07:49.229
There was nothing overtly menacing about it,
but somehow, just hearing that noise chilled

00:07:49.229 --> 00:07:52.460
me deeper than any icy gust of mountain breeze.

00:07:52.460 --> 00:07:57.879
It’s difficult to describe, but that terrible
sobbing felt like the purest distillation

00:07:57.879 --> 00:07:59.689
of true, bottomless despair.

00:07:59.689 --> 00:08:02.979
I’ve never heard a more hopeless and mournful
noise.

00:08:02.979 --> 00:08:07.849
Referring to his pamphlet again, Arthur speculated
that the noise could potentially be a Lynx.

00:08:07.849 --> 00:08:13.319
Such creatures were apparently native to the
area, and their calls had a quality to them.

00:08:13.319 --> 00:08:18.639
But somehow, deep down, I knew this thing
wasn’t human, and it wasn’t an animal

00:08:18.639 --> 00:08:19.639
either.

00:08:19.639 --> 00:08:23.529
Whatever was making those noises defied normal
metrics of categorization.

00:08:23.529 --> 00:08:28.289
To the surprise of no one, me least of all,
Kyle decided to lead the way in the direction

00:08:28.289 --> 00:08:29.990
of that awful noise.

00:08:29.990 --> 00:08:34.590
Brandon and Arthur followed him closely over
the next tundra, getting closer and closer

00:08:34.590 --> 00:08:35.590
to the sobbing.

00:08:35.590 --> 00:08:39.431
I hung back a little further, gripped by this
profound sense of dread.

00:08:39.431 --> 00:08:44.300
I didn’t want to find out what was making
this noise - I knew, deep in my guts, that

00:08:44.300 --> 00:08:48.910
if ever we found out what was making that
noise, something terrible would happen.

00:08:48.910 --> 00:08:51.200
And then, moments later, it did.

00:08:51.200 --> 00:08:53.990
I heard Kyle gasp, for the first time ever.

00:08:53.990 --> 00:08:58.040
The snow was blinding my view, and I couldn’t
make out what was actually happening, until

00:08:58.040 --> 00:09:02.310
Arthur ran back toward my direction, and through
the blanket of white snow and with a voice

00:09:02.310 --> 00:09:07.110
barely audible through his trembling said
there was a long gray creature.

00:09:07.110 --> 00:09:10.880
He said it was naked and looked kind of human
but definitely was not.

00:09:10.880 --> 00:09:11.880
He turned back in a panic.

00:09:11.880 --> 00:09:15.660
I could barely make out what my buddies were
doing ahead of me, but from the looks of it,

00:09:15.660 --> 00:09:18.020
it seemed like Kyle was walking closer.

00:09:18.020 --> 00:09:21.790
Every cell in my body was screaming, “No,
no, no!”

00:09:21.790 --> 00:09:25.050
But by that point, it was already far too
late for all of us.

00:09:25.050 --> 00:09:26.800
The creature began to turn.

00:09:26.800 --> 00:09:33.100
Perhaps it was raw animal instinct that saved
me, but I began to turn, too, almost in unison,

00:09:33.100 --> 00:09:34.410
to look away from the creature.

00:09:34.410 --> 00:09:38.221
I just knew that, whatever this thing was,
I couldn’t bear to see it.

00:09:38.221 --> 00:09:43.161
And it was at that moment, as my body hit
the snow and I forced my eyes closed, that

00:09:43.161 --> 00:09:44.710
the screaming began.

00:09:44.710 --> 00:09:50.520
First, I could recognize the voices of Brandon,
Arthur, and Kyle screaming, and then something

00:09:50.520 --> 00:09:51.520
worse.

00:09:51.520 --> 00:09:55.440
I can only assume it was the creature itself,
making the most terrible racket.

00:09:55.440 --> 00:09:57.880
Shrieking, moaning, sobbing.

00:09:57.880 --> 00:10:02.980
And then...Ripping and tearing, blood splattering
across the snow.

00:10:02.980 --> 00:10:07.530
I just cowered and shook against the ground
as a massacre unfolded behind me.

00:10:07.530 --> 00:10:11.110
I was listening to my friends die, powerless.

00:10:11.110 --> 00:10:16.370
Kyle, all his strength and direction stripped
away by terror, was snatched by the creature

00:10:16.370 --> 00:10:19.120
as freezing, snowy wind gave it a cloak.

00:10:19.120 --> 00:10:24.600
I heard Kyle scream, and then never heard
anything from him ever again.

00:10:24.600 --> 00:10:27.360
Soon enough, it was quiet once more.

00:10:27.360 --> 00:10:32.330
No noise save for the howling wind and the
quiet sobbing of the abomination from the

00:10:32.330 --> 00:10:33.330
snow.

00:10:33.330 --> 00:10:37.300
I don’t know for sure why the creature spared
me, but part of me has always suspected that

00:10:37.300 --> 00:10:39.010
it was because I never looked at it.

00:10:39.010 --> 00:10:43.920
I’ll forever be darkly curious as to what
that monster truly looked like, but my friends

00:10:43.920 --> 00:10:46.930
found out, and now I’d never see them again.

00:10:46.930 --> 00:10:51.740
I wandered through the unforgiving cold for
hours, fully expecting that my frozen body

00:10:51.740 --> 00:10:55.950
would be found in the Alps hours, days, or
even weeks later.

00:10:55.950 --> 00:10:58.160
I didn’t even care anymore.

00:10:58.160 --> 00:11:02.080
The cold numbed my skin, my limbs, and my
thoughts.

00:11:02.080 --> 00:11:06.300
At some point, a helicopter descended onto
the mountainside, full of men in tactical

00:11:06.300 --> 00:11:08.530
gear with insignias I couldn’t recognize.

00:11:08.530 --> 00:11:13.340
They wrapped me in an emergency blanket and
brought me in, telling me I required treatment.

00:11:13.340 --> 00:11:17.790
It all turned to static as we fluttered away
from the mountainside, and all I could think

00:11:17.790 --> 00:11:21.680
about was the monster that had taken my friends
from me.

00:11:21.680 --> 00:11:24.110
It was still out there, waiting.

00:11:24.110 --> 00:11:28.440
Now go check out “SCP-096 - Look at a Picture
of Shy Guy in Space?

00:11:28.440 --> 00:11:35.620
The Shy Guy Questions and Theories” and
“SCP-096 Shy Guy ESCAPE - Incident 096-1-A

00:11:35.620 --> 00:11:39.720
Containment Breach” for more on the Shy
Guy that haunts all of our dreams!

